        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Playing in Running Shoes</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Running shoes are built to roll you forward, with a raised heel and a narrow, rounded sole. On a pickleball court, a hard sideways push can slide your foot over the edge of that sole and roll your ankle. Any court shoe, even an inexpensive one, is a safer choice than the best running shoe for this sport.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying an Indoor Sole for Outdoor Play</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Gum and indoor-tuned outsoles grip gym floors beautifully but wear quickly on abrasive outdoor courts. If most of your games are at a park, choose a shoe whose outsole is described as built for hard courts, and save indoor-specific shoes for rec centre play.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sizing Up to Fix a Narrow Fit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Going up a half size to relieve pressure across the forefoot leaves extra length that lets your foot slide forward on stops, which causes black toenails and blisters. If a shoe pinches across the width, look for a 2E or wide version of the same size instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring Worn Outsoles</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A shoe can look fine on top while the forefoot tread is gone. Smooth rubber at the inside edge loses grip exactly when you push off sideways. Check the outsole every month or so, and replace the pair when the tread is smooth or the midsole shows through.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Wearing New Shoes to a Tournament</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many court shoes feel stiff out of the box, and some, like the New Balance Fresh Foam X Lav v2, are reported to need a lengthy break-in. Wear a new pair for a few practice sessions first, and keep your old pair in the bag until the new ones feel right.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

