          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What are the best pickleball shoes?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most players, we would start with the K-Swiss Express Light Pickleball, a purpose-built, lightweight court shoe that also comes in a wide version. Players with wide feet should look at the Skechers Viper Court Pro 2.0 Wide, indoor players at the ASICS Gel-Renma, and anyone on a budget at the ASICS Gel-Dedicate 8 Pickleball.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I really need pickleball shoes?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You need court shoes, but they do not have to be labelled pickleball. Tennis shoes are court shoes and work well, and two of our picks are tennis models stocked in pickleball sections. What you should avoid is running shoes, whose raised heel and narrow sole make lateral stops risky for your ankles.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I wear tennis shoes for pickleball?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Tennis shoes have the flat, wide outsole and lateral support pickleball needs. Pickleball-specific shoes are often a little lighter and lower because the sport involves less sprinting and sliding, but a tennis shoe you already own is a perfectly good choice, especially on outdoor hard courts.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What pickleball shoes are best for wide feet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The Skechers Viper Court Pro 2.0 Wide is listed in a 4E men's width and a wide D women's width, which is wider than most court shoes. The ASICS Gel-Resolution X and New Balance 696 v6 are stocked in men's 2E, and the K-Swiss Express Light comes in a wide version.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are indoor and outdoor pickleball shoes different?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Often, yes. Outdoor shoes use harder, more durable rubber to survive abrasive hard courts, while indoor shoes use softer compounds, sometimes gum rubber, for grip on wood. An indoor sole wears quickly outdoors, so buy for where you play most, and check that indoor shoes are non-marking.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should I replace pickleball shoes?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Replace them when the forefoot tread is worn smooth, when you can see midsole through the outsole, or when the shoe feels less stable or your feet ache more than usual after play. Regular outdoor players often wear through a pair within a season; indoor players usually get longer.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What pickleball shoes are best for plantar fasciitis?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Look for generous cushioning and a firm, supportive heel counter, such as the New Balance Fresh Foam X Lav v2, and consider a supportive insole. A shoe can make play more comfortable, but it is not a treatment. If heel or arch pain persists, see a podiatrist before continuing to play through it.
              </p>
            </div>

